class:: Inharmonicity
summary:: Power-weighted deviation from an integer harmonic series
categories:: UGens>Analysis>Tonal
related:: Guides/MIR2, Classes/HPCP, Classes/HarmonicPeaks

description::
Shared interpolated spectral peaks are associated with the nearest integer harmonic of the supplied F0. Only the strongest matching peak occupies each harmonic slot. Missing harmonics retain zero slots; an incorrect F0 or tight tolerance changes the result. The formulas are explicit MIR2 conventions in the harmonic-descriptor tradition summarized by Peeters (2004); this is independent code, not a wrapper around another analysis library. The value is sum(a_h squared * abs(f_h-h*f0)) / (f0 * sum(a_h squared)). Increasing partial stretch increases it while associated partials remain within tolerance. This is a dimensionless frequency deviation; unmatched peaks are excluded.



classmethods::
method:: kr
argument:: chain
FFT chain. This analyzer updates when a new frame arrives and holds its output between frames.

argument:: f0
Supplied fundamental in Hz, for example the first output of Pitch2. Nonpositive or invalid values disable harmonic association.

argument:: maxHarmonics
Literal number of harmonic slots from 1 through 64. Harmonic index is stable when partials are missing.

argument:: tolerance
Maximum distance in cents between a peak and its nearest integer multiple of f0, clamped from 0 through 600. Default 50.

argument:: peakThreshold
Minimum local-peak magnitude relative to the largest included spectral peak, from 0 through 1. Up to 128 strongest peaks are retained.

argument:: fftSize
Literal power of two from 256 through 8192, matching the FFT buffer. Frequency resolution and first-result latency depend on this size.

returns::
Nonnegative inharmonicity; zero for an exact series or no associated partials.

method:: fromAudio
Convenience constructor: accepts audio and builds Pitch2 plus FFT internally, then calls kr with the estimated F0. The input range defaults to 55 through 1760 Hz, FFT size 4096 and hop fraction 0.25; it accepts the same harmonic controls. Use kr when sharing an existing pitch estimate or FFT chain.

examples::
Inharmonicity measures the power-weighted deviation of spectral partials from ideal integer multiples of a fundamental. In this example, it drives an adaptive dispersive comb resonator: high inharmonicity from acoustic bell and kalimba plucks increases allpass delay dispersion, transforming harmonic resonances into metallic, inharmonic timbres. An interactive code::MIR2GUI.scope:: tracks the real-time inharmonicity index.
code::
(
Routine({
    var path = MIR2GUI.soundPath("plucks.wav");
    ~mir2Buffer = if(path.notNil) { Buffer.read(s, path) } { nil };
    ~mir2Bus = Bus.control(s, 1);
    s.sync;
    ~mir2Example = {
        var source = PlayBuf.ar(1, ~mir2Buffer, BufRateScale.kr(~mir2Buffer), loop: 1);
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);

        var pitch = PitchMPM.kr(source, 100, 1600, 2048, 0.25);
        var chain = FFT(LocalBuf(2048), source, 0.25);
        var inharm = Inharmonicity.kr(chain, pitch[0], maxHarmonics: 16, tolerance: 150, fftSize: 2048);
        var inharmSmooth = inharm.lag(0.04);

        // Inharmonic dispersion comb resonator
        var delayTime = (pitch[0].clip(100, 1200).reciprocal).lag(0.02);
        var comb = CombC.ar(source, 0.05, delayTime, 0.3 + (inharmSmooth * 0.8).clip(0, 1.5));
        var sound = (source * 0.25) + (comb * 0.2);

        if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { Out.kr(~mir2Bus, inharm) };
        sound * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.2) ! 2
    }.play;

    ~mir2GUI = MIR2GUI.scope("Inharmonicity Index", ~mir2Bus, 0.0, 0.5);
}).play(AppClock);
)
::
code::
(
~mir2Example.free;
if(~mir2GUI.notNil) { ~mir2GUI.close };
if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { ~mir2Bus.free };
if(~mir2Buffer.notNil) { ~mir2Buffer.free };
)
::

